## Exercise Steps

### Step 1: Create the User Context

Choose one of two approaches:

#### Option A: Automated Fast Path (`./create-user-context.sh`)

Run the provided script to generate private keys, issue a client certificate via Kubernetes CSR, and register
credentials and context in `~/.kube/config`:

```bash
./create-user-context.sh
```

This performs the following actions:

1. Generates an RSA private key (`{{ cka_lab_rbac_user | default('vagrant') }}.key`).
1. Creates and approves a Kubernetes `CertificateSigningRequest` for Subject `/CN={{ cka_lab_rbac_user |
   default('vagrant') }}`.
1. Retrieves and saves the signed client certificate (`{{ cka_lab_rbac_user | default('vagrant') }}.crt`).
1. Adds credentials and context `{{ cka_lab_rbac_user | default('vagrant') }}` to `~/.kube/config`.

#### Option B: Hands-on Manual Exam Drill (`manual-user/`)

In the actual CKA exam, helper scripts are not available. Practice executing every step manually:

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_rbac_dir }}/manual-user
```

##### 1. Generate Private Key and CSR via OpenSSL

Generate a 2048-bit RSA private key and a Certificate Signing Request with Subject `/CN={{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user |
default('developer') }}/O={{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_group | default('developers') }}`:

```bash
# Generate private key
openssl genrsa -out {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.key 2048
chmod 0600 {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.key

# Generate Certificate Signing Request (CSR)
openssl req -new -key {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.key \
  -out {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.csr \
  -subj "/CN={{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}/O={{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_group | default('developers') }}"

# Verify CSR subject
openssl req -in {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.csr -noout -subject
```

##### 2. Create and Submit Kubernetes CertificateSigningRequest (CSR)

Encode the CSR file as a single-line base64 string and submit a `CertificateSigningRequest` object:

```bash
# Encode CSR into a single base64 string
CSR_BASE64=$(base64 < {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.csr | tr -d "\r\n")

# Submit CertificateSigningRequest manifest
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}
spec:
  request: ${CSR_BASE64}
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
  expirationSeconds: 86400
  usages:
  - client auth
EOF

# Verify CSR is in Pending state
kubectl get csr {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}
```

##### 3. Approve CSR and Extract Signed Client Certificate

Approve the CSR and decode the issued certificate from `.status.certificate`:

```bash
# Approve the pending CSR
kubectl certificate approve {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}

# Confirm condition shows Approved,Issued
kubectl get csr {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}

# Extract and base64-decode the signed client certificate
kubectl get csr {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }} \
  -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.crt
chmod 0644 {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.crt

# Verify certificate Subject and Issuer
openssl x509 -in {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.crt -noout -subject -issuer
```

##### 4. Register Credentials and Context in `~/.kube/config`

Configure the user credentials and a dedicated context:

```bash
# Detect cluster name (default: kubernetes)
CLUSTER_NAME=$(kubectl config view --minify -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].name}')
CLUSTER_NAME="${CLUSTER_NAME:-kubernetes}"

# Embed client credentials in ~/.kube/config
kubectl config set-credentials {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }} \
  --client-key={{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.key \
  --client-certificate={{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}.crt \
  --embed-certs=true

# Create context mapping cluster and user
kubectl config set-context {{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}@${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --cluster="${CLUSTER_NAME}" \
  --user={{ cka_lab_rbac_manual_user | default('developer') }}

# Verify context registration
kubectl config get-contexts
```

##### 5. Automated Verification

Execute the verification script to confirm all keys, certificates, and contexts match CKA specifications:

```bash
./verify-manual-user.sh
```

